[59.259315, "o", "PASSAGE_0034: 8.5 Geriatric Use\r\nForty-one of the 4234 patients in double-blind, placebo-controlled, clinical trial\r\nPASSAGE_0035: 8.6 Renal Impairment\r\nAcamprosate calcium is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment\r\nPASSAGE_0036: 10 OVERDOSAGE\r\nIn all reported cases of acute overdosage with acamprosate calcium (total reported dos\r\nPASSAGE_0037: 11 DESCRIPTION\r\nAcamprosate calcium delayed-release tablets are supplied as an enteric-coated tablet \r\nPASSAGE_0038: 12.1 Mechanism of Action\r\nThe mechanism of action of acamprosate in maintenance of alcohol abstinence\r\nPASSAGE_0039: 12.2 Pharmacodynamics\r\nPharmacodynamic studies have shown that acamprosate calcium reduces alcohol in\r\nPASSAGE_0040: 12.3\tAbsorption\r\nThe absolute bioavailability of acamprosate calcium after oral administration is abo\r\nPASSAGE_0041: The volume of distribution for acamprosate following intravenous administration is estimated to be 7\r\nPASSAGE_0042: Acamprosate does not undergo metabolism.\r\nPASSAGE_0043: After oral dosing of 2 x 333 mg of acamprosate calcium, the terminal half-life ranges from approxima\r\nPASSAGE_0044: Gender:\r\nAcamprosate calcium does not exhibit any significant pharmacokinetic differences between mal\r\nPASSAGE_0045: Acamprosate had no inducing potential on the cytochrome CYP1A2 and 3A4 systems, and in vitro inhibit\r\nPASSAGE_0046: 13.1 Carcinogenesis, Mutagenesis, Impairment of Fertility\r\nDietary administration of acamprosate calc\r\nPASSAGE_0047: 14 CLINICAL STUDIES\r\nThe efficacy of acamprosate calcium in the maintenance of abstinence was support\r\nPASSAGE_0048: 16 HOW SUPPLIED/STORAGE AND HANDLING\r\nAcamprosate calcium delayed-release tablets, 333 mg are white t\r\n"]
